Begin an interaction with the person and the task being attempted (@computing-trust-task: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Make-pending-confirmed-and-unknown-outcomes-mean-different-things.lingua#r_00336329746CC5325F937E4856") { r_00DB108C84234052A2E708BF49
An algorithm can compute its specified answer while the surrounding tool fails the person using it. Begin with a task: what change does the person need, in what situation, and how will they know it happened? The task is not yet a button sequence. Reuse [Compose procedure contracts with explicit effects and failures](Compose-procedure-contracts-with-explicit-effects-and-failures.lingua#r_00AC0C2D678AAF4FEEA2AE3BA5) for the system contract and [Define what someone should be able to understand and do](../../inquiry/core/Define-what-someone-should-be-able-to-understand-and-do.lingua#r_0070DDB221FF1E371FDC6FCBDA) for a precise account of what someone should be able to do.

Consider an original training scenario. A coordinator wants to record two accepted practice pieces in the alpha ledger, once, and know the resulting total. A reviewer needs to inspect an already committed result without adding pieces. A participant may use a keyboard, a pointing device or an assistive interface; may be interrupted; and may encounter a lost network reply. These are stipulated design cases, not observations of actual people. The ledger is a simulation, not physical inventory or payment. Asking a participant to operate this example would require a separate, described evaluation.

Describe the essential task as choosing the intended ledger, expressing the amount, reviewing the proposed change, authorizing submission, and interpreting the resulting evidence. A concrete design could use a labeled amount field and a review screen; another could import a prepared list. Both owe the same distinction between a proposal and an accepted operation. A default of alpha can make entry faster while increasing wrong-ledger errors if the person expected beta. A fast interaction is not successful when its effect differs from the intended one.

[User Interface Design and Implementation MIT task analysis and visibility](../sources/User-Interface-Design-and-Implementation-MIT-task-analysis-and-visibility.lingua#r_00344623022A36BF7031B5BCAE) supplies selected task-analysis and feedback readings. Reconstruct the task-analysis distinction in a small table: purpose, required information, present interface step, and alternative realization. For example, finding a previous receipt is the purpose; scrolling through every line is one possible realization. A search result still needs enough object and operation context to identify the right receipt. Treat the table as a hypothesis to challenge with actual work, not a substitute for observing it.

**Work:** write the coordinator and reviewer tasks without naming widgets. Identify which assumptions came from the scenario and which would require interviews or observations. Describe one interruption, one ambiguous term and one input constraint. Specify success, a serious error and a recoverable difficulty for each task. Reuse [Turn an intended use into requirements and observable evidence](../../engineering/core/Turn-an-intended-use-into-requirements-and-observable-evidence.lingua#r_0C3988C75ECA342B8BFC943458) to turn each proposed requirement into observable evidence. Keep preference, task performance and permission as separate questions: liking an interface neither proves the task completed nor grants authority to change the ledger.

**Continue:** [Make pending confirmed and unknown outcomes mean different things](Make-pending-confirmed-and-unknown-outcomes-mean-different-things.lingua#r_00336329746CC5325F937E4856).

**Return:** [Computing](../Computing.lingua#r_02FE84C805C79A9D396F478641).
